TITLE:: ShimmerView
summary:: the shimmer's display: the steps it climbs
categories:: Libraries>Reverbs
related:: Classes/Shimmer, Classes/ReverbView, Classes/HallView, Classes/PlateView, Classes/CoilView

DESCRIPTION::
The display of link::Classes/Shimmer::'s window. What a shimmer does is change pitch, so it is drawn as pitch against time, in semitones from the input: the input at 0, then a step up (or down) by the interval every round, a round being the rise plus the tank's own time. Each step is fainter by the feedback, by the tank's decay over a round, and by what the damping takes from a higher pitch; it fades out along its line. The hatched ceiling is the damping and the hatched floor the low cut: they are what stop a climb or a fall. When a round loses less than 3 dB (feedback high, decay long against the rise), the readout says it climbs until the damping stops it. A strip underneath has the level the model expects, with the output drawn over it.

table::
## the first step || drag along: the rise; up and down: the interval
## the ceiling || drag up and down: the damping
## the floor || drag up and down: the low cut
## the end of the input's line || drag along: the decay
## anywhere else || along the decay, up and down the interval
## the wheel || the feedback
::

With no feedback there is no climb: only the input's line is left, and the pitch, detune, rise and haze knobs dim. Double click a handle for its defaults. link::Classes/ReverbView:: has the rest.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: intervalText
An interval in words, as the readout says it.
ARGUMENT:: st
Semitones.
code::
ShimmerView.intervalText(7);
::

EXAMPLES::

code::
s = Shimmer.new;
s.gui;
s.feedback = 0.95; s.decay = 14;	// runs away until the damping stops it
::
